The Sixteenth China-Japan Energy and Environmental Comprehensive Forum was held in Beijing; He Li Feng attended the main forum and delivered a keynote speech - Xinhua News Agency
Xinhua News Agency reported that the Sixteenth Forum on Comprehensive Environmental and Energy Conservation between China and Japan was held in Beijing via online and offline channels on February 11th.
Central Committee Politburo Member and Director of the National Development and Reform Commission Hu Leifeng attended the main forum and delivered a keynote speech.
Huang Lifeng noted that hosting this forum is an important step in implementing the consensus reached at the joint meeting between President Xi Jinping and Prime Minister Fumio Kishida in Bangkok to strengthen green development cooperation between China and Japan.
China will fully implement the spirit of the 20th CPC National Congress and accelerate green transformation in its development model, deepen environmental pollution control, comprehensively strengthen resource conservation, actively and steadily advance carbon peak and neutrality, coordinate efforts to reduce emissions, improve air quality, expand greening, and boost growth, and promote ecological priority, efficient use of resources, and low-carbon development.
China is willing to work with Japan to deepen cooperation in green and low-carbon industries, technology, policy dialogue, and people-to-people exchanges, driving further progress in bilateral green development cooperation.
Japanese Minister of Economy, Trade and Industry Shunji Watanabe attended the main forum and delivered a keynote speech.
The forum is jointly organized by the National Development and Reform Commission of China, the Ministry of Commerce of China, the Ministry of Economy and Trade of Japan, and the Japanese-Chinese Economic Association, featuring a main forum and four sub-forums on energy efficiency improvement, automotive electrification and automation, hydrogen energy, and long-term trade between China and Japan.
Representatives from both governments and enterprises in China and Japan exchanged views.
Q&A on Double Ninth Festival | How to Interpret the Unexpectedly Strong Non-Farm Payrolls Data in January and Its Future Trends - Economic Observer - A Professional Financial News Website
Q: How do you view the unexpectedly strong January non-farm employment data from the US and its future trends?
A: On February 3rd, the US Labor Department released its January non-farm payroll data for 2023, with an increase of 5.17 million new jobs, significantly higher than market expectations of 1.89 million; unemployment rate fell to 3.4%, marking the lowest level in nearly five decades; hourly wage growth was up 4.4% year-over-year and 0.3% month-over-month, showing some moderation.
From a total perspective, this data is subject to many one-off and technical factors of disturbance, such as teachers returning from strike, adjustments in survey statistics, updates on seasonal adjustment factors, etc., which may be revised (refer to the downward revision of non-farm payroll employment for January 2022 from 5.04 million to 3.64 million). Non-farm payroll employment may not be as strong as it appears in the data.
From an organizational perspective, high-tech layoffs continue to have an impact, albeit at lower rates; but sectors with high contact levels such as leisure hotels and education/healthcare saw significant increases in new employment, indicating that there is still a labor shortage in services.
The rebound in services PMI and job vacancies/labor shortages data, combined with strong consumer credit data, suggests that US services have quickly recovered after extreme weather and holidays.
We believe that improving US labor market tightness without a recession is unlikely.
Currently, there is still a supply gap of over 5 million in US labor markets, with labor participation rates rising slowly, especially among those aged 25 to 54 who have already reached their pre-pandemic peak.
Moreover, because of strong excess saving and consumption credit among US residents, the strength of consumer demand will further delay the impact on employment markets from tightening policies.
A tight labor market in the US will be the bedrock of 2023 US economic performance.
Adding to the near-end of the tightening cycle and with inflation expectations constantly fluctuating, we do not expect sustained declines in wage growth.
Expectations for an overly optimistic path for US Fed rate hikes were already high before today’s data release.
With inflation data improving, the Fed is shortening its tightening pace while maintaining market expectations of an unstable balance between hawkishness and dovishness. But markets ignore the Fed’s hawkish tone, expecting it to cut rates twice at year-end instead of just once.
In an environment of labor market tightness and unclear tightening policy impact, we believe that the Fed’s guidance for an end-of-year rate hike is reasonable; extreme expectations in the market and crowded trading may cause some harm to the market.
While this is not the case, stubborn wage growth has continued to impede the rapid decline in inflation that was observed recently. As M2 data turns negative and leading indicators of various CPI components gradually improve, US CPI data have turned towards a quick decline phase since October. Wage growth’s stubbornness will only provide support for future inflation data; current market expectations cannot be refuted. In the next round of inflation data releases, the market may enter another round of speculation.
Shenzhen: A Leading and World-Class Fast-Paced Pioneer City - China News Service
Xinhua News Agency reported on February 11th that the Shenzhen Industrial and Information Technology Bureau has recently issued the “Action Plan for Building an Ultra-fast Pioneer City of Shenzhen”.
It is proposed that by the end of 2023, a super broadband network with high rates, large capacity, and low latency will be built to achieve the goal of “Dual Gigabit, Full Optical Network, 1 millisecond, Everything Connected,” making it China’s first and world-leading city in terms of ultra-fast speed.
The plan calls for accelerating the upgrade of the gigabit optical network.
By the end of 2023, 10G PON ports will account for 100% and there will be over 100 all-optical industrial parks (industrial parks).
The plan calls for an overall improvement in 5G network efficiency.
Developing quality specifications and standards for 5G network services in Shenzhen, conducting tests and evaluations of 5G networks, and enhancing their service quality.
Supporting basic telecommunications operators to enhance 5G network optimization in key areas and venues nationwide, with an additional 10,000 new base stations added this year, achieving an average download speed of 500 Mbps and an upload speed of 100 Mbps across the city, as well as speeds of 1 Gbps for key areas and 200 Mbps for uploads; network speeds and user experience have significantly improved.
Build a city-level high-quality wireless local area network.
Develop WLAN service standards for public places and promote upgrades in WLANs in densely populated and information-hungry areas across all districts to provide gigabit access capabilities.
Based on “Deep iNet”, a unified authentication and supervision platform for public wireless local area networks across the city is being constructed to achieve one-time authentication with seamless roaming and intelligent switching, supporting operators to integrate social self-built APs (wireless access points) with the authentication supervision platform.
By the end of 2023, we will upgrade and connect to at least 20,000 APs.
In addition, the plan calls for building high-quality optical transport networks with full spectrum capabilities; constructing multi-level computing resource supply systems; accelerating key communication network construction; and enhancing internet application service levels.
The plan clearly states that collaborative construction of an IoT perception system will be carried out.
Explore building management models for urban perception terminal construction based on the “open-source HarmonyOS” operating system to lower integration costs and accelerate the development of city-level IoT perception platforms. Promote interconnectivity and data interoperability among various municipal IoT platform networks and professional IoT platform networks.
Deepen application of IoT perception in urban management, public services, public safety, and traffic travel, expand fixed sensing channels such as city roads, buildings, and public facilities, and mobile sensing channels such as intelligent connected vehicles.
By the end of 2023, more than 9 million water, electricity, and gas IoT sensing terminals will be deployed, with the aim to launch 20 demonstration projects for multi-functional smart poles.
The plan also noted that efforts will be made to strengthen satellite-ground communication integration applications, lay out low-altitude network infrastructure construction, and promote deep coverage of 5G networks at sea.
At the same time, we will advance 5G network deep coverage at sea, build an innovation ecosystem for industry development, and support hosting top-level events and activities.
(Cai News Finance)
China’s e-commerce industry is witnessing “new variables,” how to achieve “content supremacy”?
China News Service
China News Service, February 11th: New Variables in China's E-commerce Industry - How to Achieve "Content Dominance" in Douyin?
Xia Bin, a reporter from China Finance News
We entered the e-commerce game late, but from the start we realized that standing out among millions of shops is important, and content plays a big role.
Fangfang, an 90s girl from Guangdong who runs a health food store on Douyin e-commerce, has made her offline business that has been around for over three decades blossom online.
Imitating the old Hong Kong film look and adding classic Cantonese songs, click on @Fangfang's One-Dish Wok account to see stunning videos that will leave you breathless.
At the recent Douyin E-commerce “Find Your Peers · Showcase of High-Quality Content Sharing” event, Fangfang, as an example of a high-quality creator, gave a speech on her daily creative experiences and insights.
She said on stage: "The carefully polished video has attracted much attention from netizens, and many people are interested in my family's ingredients, wanting to make their own food together; sales have opened up immediately."
Translation requested.
Fang Fang’s video of making soups has helped more people fall in love with “Guangdong flavors,” and one livestream sold as many as 300, 000 pieces of tonics for her family-run store that was on the verge of closing down.
"Without content, no e-commerce," as video and live-streaming-based e-commerce continues to develop, people's expectations for and standards of online shopping have entered a new phase.
In the early days of live-streaming e-commerce, price advantage was the sole selling point as sellers competed on low prices to attract buyers.
In recent years, there have been both phenomenon-level influencers like Lao Jiong Hong and Dongfang Zhenxuan, as well as many non-lauded experts and merchants in niche vertical fields who have emerged, leveraging their ability to consistently create high-quality content that aligns with their brand’s unique style to enhance long-term business benefits.
Gao Yaxuan from Douyin E-commerce Content Creator Operations said that in the past year, 6.27 million creators have monetized their content through Douyin E-commerce, with their creation volume increasing by over 250% and total transaction value rising more than twice.
Behind these numbers lies an author ecosystem that is burgeoning and full of vitality.
High Sky轩 said.
The Beijing Normal University Platform for Innovation in Communication and Future Media previously released the ECHI Report on the Content Ecological Health Index of the E-commerce Industry, which analyzed the importance of high-quality content from a data perspective, assessing the content ecological status of 27 major e-commerce platforms domestically using an index system.
According to the report on e-commerce industry content ecosystem health index rankings, Douyin E-commerce, Kuaishou, Tmall, Pinduoduo, and JD rank among the top five.
The report argues that e-commerce content ecology is a comprehensive concept and involves integrating e-commerce with content but not all content can be integrated with e-commerce. High-quality PGC (professional production content) and UGC (user-generated content) are better carriers for integration with e-commerce than other types of content. The higher the value of the content platform, the more diverse consumption and promotion scenarios will become, making it easier to integrate e-commerce, increasing its commercial value, and this ratio of e-commerce content production and consumption in public and private domains will also increase accordingly.
On-site, Professor Yu Guoming of Beijing Normal University’s School of Journalism and Communication, who is also chairman of the China Media Economics and Management Professional Committee, provided an analysis and interpretation of the report.
He said that in terms of content value, the current e-commerce industry has transitioned from “accessibility to consumption” to “quality-oriented consumption,” and high-quality, professional, and differentiated content is the “hard currency” of the e-commerce industry.
At the same time, content has become the new stage of e-commerce development and its fundamental and lever, with PGC (Professional Content Generator) content such as knowledge payment, specialized knowledge, and KOLs (Key Opinion Leaders) becoming standard features in live-streaming on e-commerce platforms.
Looking to the future of e-commerce, Yu Guoming proposes two suggestions.
One is to improve content structure from an angle of promoting the overall health and quality improvement of the e-commerce content ecosystem, establishing positive standards for content creation and management, and achieving dynamic equilibrium in the index of the healthy system of the e-commerce industry's content ecosystem.
Another is to look at it from an external ecosystem perspective and achieve healthy development of content e-commerce and value collaboration and creation with the external environment.
"More data and support from artificial intelligence will be needed in the future e-commerce industry to predict stability for its development; moreover, it is essential to provide more standardized and healthy growth spaces for merchants so that enterprises, users, products, and services can achieve value synergy within an eco-system of healthy content."
"(" (complete)
The US administration will blacklist six Chinese entities suspected of being involved in the spy balloon program this Friday (February 10).
The US Department of Commerce said that these five Chinese companies and one research institute helped modernize China’s armed forces, especially in aerospace projects such as blimps and aircraft.
Companies on the blacklist have difficulty obtaining US technology exports.
The list is called an “entity list,” and companies that enter it will be unable to purchase technology or goods from US suppliers unless they obtain a special export license from the US Department of Commerce.
The companies listed on the blacklist are: Beijing Nanjiang Sky Space Technology Co., Ltd., China Electronics Science and Technology Group Fourth Eighth Research Institute, Dongguan Lingkong Remote Sensing Technology Co., Ltd., Eagle Gate Aviation Technology Group Co., Ltd., Guangzhou Tianhai Xiang Aeronautical Technology Co., Ltd., and Shanxi Eagle Gate Aviation Technology Group Co., Ltd.
Prior to this, the White House had indicated it was considering broader measures “to expose and address” larger-scale surveillance activities that threaten American national security by China.
The Balloons Incident has further strained relations between China and the US, with Secretary of State Blinken cancelling his visit to Beijing.
On February 4, the US shot down an air balloon, and China raised a serious protest to the US embassy in Beijing over America’s severe interference and damage to both sides’ efforts and progress toward stabilizing Sino-US relations.
Based on existing debris evidence and information from China’s military procurement platform, the US government concluded that the manufacturer of the balloon “is directly related to the People's Liberation Army,” and was approved as a supplier by the Chinese military.
Foreign Ministry Spokesperson Ma Ning said on Friday that China has repeatedly explained and clarified its position regarding an incident where a Chinese unmanned airship without people accidentally entered US airspace due to force majeure.
According to the Foreign Ministry website, she added that US Congress resolutions are pure political maneuvering and hype.
China is strongly dissatisfied and resolutely opposes this.
Axelrod, an Assistant Secretary of Commerce for Export Enforcement at the US Department of Commerce, said that "Today's action demonstrates our efforts to identify and prevent China from using spy balloons, which have violated airspace over 40 countries including the United States."
Translation requested.
Reuters reports that an employee of Tianhai Xiang Aviation Technology Co., Ltd. in Guangzhou said he was unaware of sanctions and declined to comment further.
The Chinese embassy in the United States and other sources have not commented on these reports.
(Reuters et al.)
