Singapore’s tourism sector is experiencing a strong recovery with inbound tourists up 19 times compared to 2021.
(Singapore Tourism Board announces that international visitors to Singapore in 2023 will number between 12 million and 14 million, generating approximately S$18 billion to S$21 billion in tourism revenue; visitor numbers and tourism revenues will return to around 67-75% of pre-COVID levels in 2019; there is great likelihood for full recovery of the tourism sector in 2024.)
The Singapore tourism market gradually reopens and recovers in 2022.
The latest statistics released by the Singapore Tourism Board show that inbound tourists in 2022 grew 19-fold from 330, 000 in 2021 to 6. 3 million, surpassing the earlier forecast of 40-60 million. The number of visitors has recovered to 33% of pre-pandemic levels.
Tourism revenue in Singapore for January to September 2022 was S$8.96 billion, and it is expected that tourism revenue for the full year of 2022 will be between S$13.8 billion and S$14.3 billion, up sixfold to sevenfold from S$1.89 billion in 2021, which has already recovered to around 50% to 52% of pre-COVID-19 levels.
The Singapore government is taking multiple measures to accelerate tourism recovery.
In April 2022, the government announced that it would allocate around $490 million to stimulate tourism recovery.
The Tourism Board announced yesterday that it plans to allocate S$100 million from this budget over the next two years for more commercial and leisure activities aimed at attracting tourists back.
To make wellness tourism an important highlight of Singapore’s tourism offerings, the Singapore Tourism Board launched an intention solicitation on November 23, 2022, with plans to develop a wellness destination in the southern coast of Singapore.
Singapore Tourism Board’s Sports and Wellness Tourism Division Director Wang Lingli said that in recent years, more tourists have placed health at the forefront of all considerations.
Developing a health and wellness attraction in the South Beach area, with its Marina Bay Sands and Marina Barrages, has the potential to contribute to Singapore’s tourism and economy, encouraging visitors to spend more time there.
Tourism局局长 Chan Jianlong said that in the coming years, health and wellness will become an important component of Singapore’s tourism products.
The pandemic has raised public awareness and demand for physical and mental health.
In this regard, in the past two years, the tourism bureau has been promoting health and wellness programs for local residents and international tourists.
In June 2022, Singapore Tourism Board held its first Wellness Festival, offering over 130 health-related activities and experiences to the public.
Singapore will continue to offer new travel experiences for tourists.
In addition to new attractions such as Singapore’s Children’s Museum, Avatar World at Sentosa Bay Gardens, Central Beach Market in Sentosa, and the newly opened outdoor theatre of Night Safari, the Tourism Board will support development and updates for projects like the aviary park at Wild Safari, making Singapore an attractive destination for international travel.
According to the plan, Singapore will develop industry standards for sustainable development of meetings, exhibitions and incentive tourism in 2023, with particular focus on waste management and carbon emissions reduction issues, further optimizing the travel environment.
Tourism in Singapore is fueling an upturn in aviation.
Data shows that as of December 18, 2022, Changi Airport has seen a strong recovery in passenger traffic, with daily passengers reaching over 150,000 and weekly numbers around 1 million, equivalent to about 75% of pre-pandemic levels, and the number of employees back at work is also up to approximately 90% of their pre-pandemic level.
Data show that in January to October 2022, Singapore welcomed 23.6 million visitors, equivalent to 42% of pre-pandemic levels and 53% of pre-pandemic air traffic volumes.
Tourism’s rapid rebound has also spurred recovery in sectors such as accommodation, transportation, dining, and shopping.
The Singapore government and tourism industry have high hopes for China market.
Tourism Board’s Assistant Director for International Affairs Koh Shudan said that a survey of China travel platforms revealed Singapore’s safety index to be among the top three globally, which is also one reason why Singapore enjoys popularity with Chinese tourists.
Before the pandemic, China welcomed about 3 million visitors to Singapore for travel and tourism every year.
With China easing border policies, it is expected that this year’s number of inbound tourists will recover to 30-60% of the level in 2019.
Ko Shudan noted that the Tourism Bureau will closely monitor signals from China on further liberalization of outbound tourism policies to respond to changes in tourist demand.
The Singapore Tourism Board will also make preparations to welcome Chinese tourists who visit from overseas in response to new trends emerging in China’s outbound tourism market.
Looking ahead to 2023, Singapore Tourism Board is optimistic about the tourism market, believing that as long as global economic conditions do not experience significant downturns and there are no new large-scale outbreaks of deadly pandemics, the pace of tourism recovery will be faster than expected.
Quick Guide to Internet Finance
This book is very shallow; it can be considered an introductory course that will help you quickly understand the broad picture of internet finance but to really delve deeper, you need to follow the map and dig on your own.
And given that it was published earlier, and the content about later on in the war between Alipay and WeChat Pay for subsidies has not been mentioned yet, the share of third-party payments now is unlikely to be as small as what is stated in the book.
Nowadays, many people take their phones out to eat instead of carrying cash, and banks have also abandoned UnionPay due to high bank transaction volumes.
But still, I am very grateful to the author for writing this introductory book; it is evident that the author has had many years of financial observation experience and is familiar with both domestic and international financial development histories, which allows him/her to write in such an easy-to-understand manner.
Writing highbrow is easy; making it accessible is hard.
