I think it’s acceptable; first of all, it is relatively cheap and divided into two volumes with over seven hundred pages, which makes them easier to read as hardcover editions; secondly, unlike Michelangelo’s Complete Works, this book focuses more on paintings than manuscripts, with Leonardo da Vinci’s black-and-white sketches taking up most of the space for readers with limited budgets or those not interested in art history.
2. The commentary on the painting is excellent; it includes analysis of composition, sketches, and historical background (e.g., The Last Supper), but I am unsure whether this was due to printing or book size issues, as some shadows were too dark and difficult to see details, which is somewhat disappointing.
3. Compared to Michelangelo’s sketches, Da Vinci’s drawings are far more detailed.
