The first factor that determines an employee’s job satisfaction is not salary but whether they feel sufficiently valued and appreciated in their work.
According to Maslow’s hierarchy of needs theory, after basic survival needs have been met, the most urgent need is the fulfillment of psychological needs – the desire for understanding, affirmation, and recognition.
Indeed, for the Chinese people, most of them “love you in my heart but can’t say it out loud.” Even if they have love, gratitude, and admiration, they keep these feelings inside without saying anything.
The author in this book explains five kinds of praise language: affirming words, thoughtful moments, service behavior, satisfactory gifts, and physical contact.
I think it is more important that you can express appreciation in these five languages without reservation and with sincerity whenever you encounter people around you, whether they be family members, friends, or colleagues.
Actually, the more closely we get to someone, the more we need appreciation and gratitude from them, making our lives better.
Appreciation can help companies retain talent to some extent and reduce turnover rates.
Thank you to the translator for bringing us exciting ideas from abroad and around the world.
Due to cultural backgrounds in China and Western countries, there is likely to be some difference between languages, so if you have an opportunity to see the original version of the author, I believe you will gain more.
Once again, thank you to the authors and translators as well as editors for their hard work!
A Customer's Testimonial
If you receive such defective items, Amazon customer service tells you not to worry and that they will contact you within about five days.
Then you contact customer service personnel to be told that this issue will be handled by an expert and he/she will inform you of the result.
You can never reach this specialist either by phone or email; you have no idea of her ID number and there is no direct line to contact her. It’s just that she will call you if you don’t call her.
Tragedy is that I waited for nearly three days, and this official called me; however, my phone was away from me for a while, so I did not receive his call.
I waited for two hours without getting that specialist's call; I then contacted Amazon customer service and sent an email to them asking her to call me back.
It has been four days since I received this defective coffee maker from Amazon and still don’t know what kind of treatment it will receive!
Five US Amazon products have no issues; overseas shopping is good, but comparing with Amazon's service it is miles away.
Six is not enough for me; I can only continue to wait...
Not really recommending it.
A while ago I read it and just happened to see some notes on my memory about writing an读后感.
This book is more of a collection of life's love fragments than a short story anthology.
The tone of this book is extremely plain; it’s like drinking water – bland and unassuming. The language used is minimalist to an extreme degree, with every word chosen for its impact on the reader. The plot is as quiet and unassuming as a summer afternoon in the city streets.
Each short story is a snapshot of a small period in everyday life, without an opening or an ending.
Finally gathered together, they named it "When We Talk About Love, What Are We Talking About?"
I think this collection of short stories and its title represent an act of artistry; the author wants to convey that love is not merely the ups and downs on stage or the eternal vows in urban legends, nor is it just the passionate embrace between lovers. Love should also be the mundane life without passion, the uninteresting days repeating day after day, with no worries about living up to expectations, avoiding reality, and being lost in uncertainty about the future.
As you read this book, memories of arguing couples beside you, uninteresting marriages with acquaintances, and unsatisfactory relationships may come to mind.
This is why I do not recommend this book – from an aesthetic point of view, its prose and plot are uninteresting; unless your life is like a tempestuous sea or as sweet as living in a candy castle, you will find reading boring.
And in terms of depth of thought, everyday situations do not surprise or expand one’s mind and perspective (this is unlike in Gabriel Garcia Marquez’s Love in the Time of Cholera), while brief episodes fail to address any issues, such as what kind of mistake led to the failure of love?
What causes the breakdown of the protagonist's marriage?
So I don’t recommend this book; it’s not worth reading either way.
The author’s ideas are more conveyed through techniques and genres than plot, which is also one reason why this book was not as enjoyable to read.
